WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:05.742
Width reflects species, size, individual history and other influences.

00:00:06.197 --> 00:00:10.033
A raw mean alone need not isolate the signal of interest.

00:00:10.487 --> 00:00:15.289
Different widths in the same year do not automatically indicate different dates.

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:19.728
Distinguish growth level from the sequence of variation.

00:00:20.531 --> 00:00:25.460
Here, index = ring width divided by supplied growth-curve value.

00:00:25.915 --> 00:00:31.178
An index of 1 matches the curve; 1.2 is 1.2 times it.

00:00:31.639 --> 00:00:34.829
The curve choice affects the variation retained.

00:00:35.283 --> 00:00:39.803
Dividing by a reference growth curve produces a dimensionless index.

00:00:40.257 --> 00:00:44.188
The chosen reference affects which variations remain.

00:00:44.991 --> 00:00:48.492
A is indexed against two different reference curves.

00:00:48.954 --> 00:00:52.684
A flexible reference can remove longer-term variation.

00:00:53.138 --> 00:00:57.368
Record the question, method and settings; compare results.

00:00:57.822 --> 00:01:03.584
A smooth fitted curve can remove slow variation of interest as well as an age trend.

00:01:04.387 --> 00:01:08.914
Tree A has two radii: 0.8 and 1.2.

00:01:09.376 --> 00:01:12.304
Tree B has one: 1.5.

00:01:12.758 --> 00:01:15.541
Average A first: 1.0.

00:01:15.995 --> 00:01:19.554
The equally weighted tree mean is 1.25.

00:01:20.008 --> 00:01:25.521
A direct mean of all three radii is about 1.17, giving A more weight.

00:01:25.981 --> 00:01:31.087
Distinguish averaging within a tree from averaging among trees so that extra cores do not

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:34.548
unintentionally give one tree excessive weight.

00:01:35.351 --> 00:01:39.201
Average the available tree indices in each calendar year.

00:01:39.656 --> 00:01:43.399
A continuous chronology can have changing sample depth.

00:01:43.853 --> 00:01:48.906
Tree count alone is insufficient; consider agreement and sampling bias.

00:01:49.366 --> 00:01:52.899
Earlier chronology sections may have fewer trees.

00:01:53.353 --> 00:01:58.504
Read the mean together with the number of independent trees supporting each year.

00:01:59.307 --> 00:02:04.729
Calculate A, B and C indices from widths and supplied curves in the CSV.

00:02:05.191 --> 00:02:09.368
Average the available indices by calendar year and count trees n.

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:15.204
Compare 1998–2000 and consider the effect of replacing blanks with zero.

00:02:15.665 --> 00:02:20.934
Pause to calculate indices, combine within trees and then average by year.

00:02:21.388 --> 00:02:25.652
Missing observations must not be replaced with zeros.
